          "content": "# Unit 1.3: Food and biochemical processing\n\n**Chemical Engineering Fundamentals \u00b7 Unit 1: Process industries**\n\n## Purpose and measurable learning outcomes\n\nFood processing combines mixing, heating, drying, separation and microbial or enzymatic transformation. Quality can mean microbiological safety, nutrition, texture, composition, shelf life and sensory attributes. Nutrient loss and water removal change mass fractions even when absolute dry-solids mass is conserved. Batch traceability is important when feedstock changes with season.\n\nAfter completing this lesson, distinguish the principal measured variables from dependent calculations, explain the role of the selected boundary or material system, and defend the operating range in which the simplified model is credible. Use the lesson's worked calculation, then change one assumption and describe how the governing equation or interpretation changes. Do not assume that matching a single numerical result validates the entire physical process.\n\n## Technical development\n\nWater removal is a powerful example of the difference between total mass and component mass. A 1000 kg/h wet stream with 10% dry solids carries 100 kg/h solids. If the dried stream has 40% dry solids, its final total rate must be 250 kg/h, and 750 kg/h water has left. These numbers say nothing by themselves about microbial inactivation, retention of vitamins, volatile flavor losses or fouling of equipment. Drying analysis should therefore be coupled with quality controls, exposure limits and adequate accounting for any solid particles carried away by exhaust air.\n\n### Governing quantitative relationship\n\nDry solids entering = dry solids leaving when solids loss is negligible; 1000 kg at 10% solids contains 100 kg dry solids.\n\nA physically interpretable engineering calculation is written first using named quantities and units. Compute at a stated operating condition, check that every term has compatible dimensions, and then compare the answer with conservation and expected practical bounds. Record whether a quantity was measured, calculated from another measurement, specified by a manufacturer, or merely assumed for illustration.\n\n### Reasoning about model scope\n\nThe equation is deliberately narrower than a full industrial design. It can illuminate a conserved quantity, a thermodynamic trend, an equipment response or a measurement inconsistency, but omitted behaviors may dominate near phase change, rapid transients, strong chemical nonideality, severe fouling or abnormal operations. When results depend heavily on a single assumed coefficient, an engineer should gather better evidence for that coefficient instead of adding more significant digits to the output.\n\nA useful independent test asks whether the model behaves sensibly when an input approaches a limiting case. A zero feed should not generate positive material output. A concentration from two conserved mixed streams should remain in the interval bounded by those inlet concentrations. A heat-transfer model should not imply an impossible cold-to-hot flow without external work. If such a check fails, re-examine the boundary, equation and units before inspecting the software.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA dryer must bring 1000 kg/h feed from 10% to 40% solids. Compute final mass flow and water removal while preserving dry solids.\n\n**Suggested solution method.** Identify known and unknown quantities explicitly. Choose a consistent basis (for example kg/h, kmol/h or kW) and write a separate balance for every conserved component or for energy. Substitute values only after solving the symbolic relationship. Evaluate any feasibility constraint such as nonnegative inventories, a physically possible composition or a positive temperature driving force. Reconcile the answer against another observation wherever possible, and state what additional measurements would distinguish competing explanations.\n\n## Diagnostic misconception\n\n**Incorrect shortcut:** Misreading a wet-basis fraction as dry-basis moisture content.\n\nTo correct this, state precisely which simplifying assumption would be required for the shortcut to work and test that assumption against real material, property or operating data. Small spreadsheet residuals do not independently establish physical correctness. Never silently hide an unaccounted side stream, unknown phase transition or unavailable safety limit.\n\n## Practice questions with answer guidance\n\n1. **Quantitative reconstruction.** Starting from the relationship above, write the governing equation symbolically, label every variable with units, and reproduce its stated numerical example. **Answer guidance:** Every substituted term must retain its units until cancellation; report the final result on the same basis as the described process. Check the arithmetic using a second calculation, ideally a component or energy balance.\n2. **Case diagnosis.** A dryer must bring 1000 kg/h feed from 10% to 40% solids. Compute final mass flow and water removal while preserving dry solids. **Answer guidance:** Distinguish what can be calculated from the supplied data from what remains unknown. List missing properties or measurements before declaring a real operating decision justified.\n3. **Sensitivity and skepticism.** Change the most influential input by 10% while keeping other assumptions fixed. Predict whether the output increases, decreases or remains unchanged, and explain the mechanism. **Answer guidance:** Differentiate the relationship qualitatively or numerically; an output that becomes physically impossible indicates a model-limit failure, not a valid prediction.\n4. **Professional communication.** Misreading a wet-basis fraction as dry-basis moisture content. **Answer guidance:** Replace this shortcut with a version-controlled note containing correct variable definitions, relevant uncertainty, one numerical counterexample and a plan for validation.\n\n## Simulation-led learning\n\nOpen [interactive laboratory 01](../labs/lab-01.html), which exercises a related quantitative process model. Check the initial output by hand; use the exported curve to identify its dominant sensitivity. In the accompanying lab notes, distinguish which model inputs correspond to directly measurable quantities and which would require property correlations or empirical fitting. Record a falsifiable observation rather than describing the curve as simply realistic.\n\n## Further study\n\nCompare this introductory method with material-and-energy balances, chemical engineering thermodynamics or process systems analysis, as applicable. For an industrial application, consult substance-specific property measurements, current engineering standards and the site's written safety procedures. The laboratory model is for learning, not for commissioning or regulatory evidence.\n\n[\u2190 Unit overview](../units/unit-01.html) \u00b7 [Book contents](../index.html)"
