          "id": "sec-1-7",
          "secNumber": "1.7",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Process Industries and Chemical Manufacturing Value Chains",
          "content": "# Unit 1 assessment: Process industries\n\nThese 12 problems combine quantitative reproduction, model validity, and audit methodology. Answers below are intended as diagnostic reasoning, not fabricated plant data.\n\n### Question 1: analysis of chemical value chains and manufacturing\n\nA specialty chemical plant targets 15 t/d shipments and has estimated recovery 0.75. Calculate feed requirement and identify what production losses must be recorded separately.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 1.1. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 2: model validity\n\nApplying reaction conversion to plant output without accounting for separation losses and off-spec material. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 3: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: Saleable mass = feed mass \u00d7 overall isolated yield; a 20 t/d feed with 0.82 overall yield gives 16.4 t/d saleable material. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 5: analysis of fuels, energy carriers and conversion\n\nA site needs 36 GJ/h of heat, and a boiler efficiency estimate is 0.80. Estimate incoming thermal energy while identifying fuel quality uncertainty.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 1.2. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 6: model validity\n\nTreating fuel energy, electrical energy and process heat as identical useful outputs. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 7: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: If a utility delivers 18 MW of useful heat at efficiency 0.75, fuel energy input is 18/0.75 = 24 MW. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 9: analysis of food and biochemical processing\n\nA dryer must bring 1000 kg/h feed from 10% to 40% solids. Compute final mass flow and water removal while preserving dry solids.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 1.3. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 10: model validity\n\nMisreading a wet-basis fraction as dry-basis moisture content. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 11: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: Dry solids entering = dry solids leaving when solids loss is negligible; 1000 kg at 10% solids contains 100 kg dry solids. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 13: analysis of pharmaceutical processes and quality\n\nAn intermediate has acceptable chemical purity but out-of-range residual solvent. Explain why a strong yield number does not authorize release.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 1.4. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 14: model validity\n\nAssuming laboratory-scale product quality is preserved by scaling time and vessel volume proportionally. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 15: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: A 50 kg batch with 0.92 recovery yields 46 kg before final quality release; accepted output may be lower. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena."
