          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Engineering Quantities, Dimensions and Systems of Units",
          "content": "# Unit 2 assessment: Engineering quantities\n\nThese 12 problems combine quantitative reproduction, model validity, and audit methodology. Answers below are intended as diagnostic reasoning, not fabricated plant data.\n\n### Question 1: analysis of si units and dimensional consistency\n\nConvert a volumetric flow of 18 m\u00b3/h to litres per second, then convert to mass flow for density 980 kg/m\u00b3.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 2.1. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 2: model validity\n\nUsing degrees Celsius in an ideal-gas formula that requires thermodynamic kelvin. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 3: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: Dimension of dynamic viscosity is M L^-1 T^-1, consistent with Pa\u00b7s; 1 Pa\u00b7s = 1 kg/(m\u00b7s). Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 5: analysis of flow rates and concentration measures\n\nTwo liquid solutions of equal density have different solute mass fractions. Determine final fraction from solute mass, not by averaging reported percentages.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 2.2. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 6: model validity\n\nAveraging two concentrations without accounting for unequal flowrates. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 7: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: For a 100 kg/h feed containing 12 wt% solute, solute rate is 12 kg/h and solvent rate 88 kg/h. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 9: analysis of properties and reference conditions\n\nA flowmeter is calibrated using water but measures a warm organic liquid. Identify the property information needed before interpreting its indication.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 2.3. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 10: model validity\n\nSubstituting a room-temperature property in a boiling or high-pressure calculation without testing sensitivity. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 11: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: Mass flow = density \u00d7 volume flow; at 0.85 kg/L and 12 L/min the mass rate is 10.2 kg/min. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 13: analysis of estimation, uncertainty and scale\n\nAn instrument reports 0.008736 m\u00b3/s while the pump nameplate supports roughly 0.001\u20130.003 m\u00b3/s. Propose a data-reconciliation sequence.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 2.4. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 14: model validity\n\nTreating a tidy spreadsheet result as proof that field observations were correctly calibrated. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 15: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: For independent uncertainties u1 and u2, combined standard uncertainty u = sqrt(u1\u00b2+u2\u00b2); 3 and 4 units combine to 5. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena."
