          "id": "sec-3-7",
          "secNumber": "3.7",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Process Representation, Flowsheets and Degrees of Freedom",
          "content": "# Unit 3 assessment: Process representation\n\nThese 12 problems combine quantitative reproduction, model validity, and audit methodology. Answers below are intended as diagnostic reasoning, not fabricated plant data.\n\n### Question 1: analysis of streams, boundaries and system diagrams\n\nA storage tank receives 11 kg/s and discharges 9 kg/s. Calculate 15-minute mass inventory change and state the needed assumption about discharge.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 3.1. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 2: model validity\n\nApplying steady state to a tank whose level is visibly changing. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 3: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: Accumulation = input \u2013 output + generation \u2013 consumption; for conserved total mass generation and consumption are zero. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 5: analysis of process flow diagrams and stream tables\n\nCreate a stream table for a crystallizer with feed, mother liquor, wet crystals and recovered solvent. Identify measurements needed for closure.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 3.2. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 6: model validity\n\nLeaving a small vent off the flowsheet while claiming exact material-balance closure. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 7: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: For a nonreactive separator at steady state, F = P + W; if feed is 500 kg/h and product 430 kg/h, waste is 70 kg/h. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 9: analysis of batch, continuous and recycle systems\n\nFresh flow is 100 kg/h and 40% of a well-mixed loop stream is returned. Compute circulation and the stream leaving the loop.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 3.3. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 10: model validity\n\nInterpreting a recycle ratio R/F as the same parameter as a recycle fraction R/(F+R). Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 11: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: For fresh rate F and returned fraction r in a simple nonreactive loop, circulating rate is F/(1-r) when the split model is valid. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 13: analysis of instruments and operational documentation\n\nSketch a tank level loop with transmitter, controller and outlet valve. Describe the effect of lost instrument air for two possible fail positions.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 3.4. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 14: model validity\n\nAssuming a controller display is an independent safeguard against the failure of that same controller. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 15: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: For instrument span 0\u2013200 kPa, a reading of 125 kPa represents 62.5% of span when the lower-range value is zero. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena."
