          "id": "sec-4-7",
          "secNumber": "4.7",
          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Material Accounting, Conservation Principles and Recycle",
          "content": "# Unit 4 assessment: Material accounting\n\nThese 12 problems combine quantitative reproduction, model validity, and audit methodology. Answers below are intended as diagnostic reasoning, not fabricated plant data.\n\n### Question 1: analysis of conservation balances without reaction\n\nA nonreactive vessel receives 12 kg/s but 11 kg/s leaves for 30 minutes. Compute accumulation and identify an operational consequence.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 4.1. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 2: model validity\n\nConfusing disappearance of a reactant species with destruction of total mass. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 3: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: At steady state, 650 kg/h enters and two outlets carry 410 and 240 kg/h; residual closure is zero. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 5: analysis of component balances and blending\n\nBlend streams at 6 wt% and 21 wt% to reach a target 12 wt% for a fixed total 500 kg/h. Determine flow allocation using two linear balances.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 4.2. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 6: model validity\n\nReplacing a component mass balance with a simple unweighted arithmetic average. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 7: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: 80 kg at 5 wt% plus 120 kg at 20 wt% contains 4+24=28 kg solute in 200 kg total, or 14 wt%. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 9: analysis of stoichiometry, conversion and yield\n\nWith A=12 kmol/h and B=20 kmol/h for A+2B\u2192C, determine limiting reagent and C production at 80% of maximum extent.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 4.3. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 10: model validity\n\nUsing 80% conversion of A as if it automatically meant 80% selectivity to the desired product. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 11: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: For A + 2B \u2192 C with 8 mol A and 10 mol B, maximum extent is min(8,10/2)=5 mol and maximum C is 5 mol. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 13: analysis of recycle, purge and process constraints\n\nA separator returns 70% of a stream to a reactor and withdraws 30%. Identify an inert component balance and explain how an added purge changes steady composition.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 4.4. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 14: model validity\n\nTreating the material in recycle as freshly created production. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 15: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: A simple nonreactive recycle with return fraction r has circulating rate F/(1-r), so F=100 kg/h and r=0.60 gives 250 kg/h. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena."
