          "id": "sec-5-6",
          "secNumber": "5.6",
          "heading": "Process Simulation & Industrial Design Case: Energy Foundations, Enthalpy and First Law Balances",
          "content": "# Unit 5 applied engineering project \u2014 A heat-recovery demonstration system\n\n## Setting and purpose\n\nA warm process stream transfers energy to a colder process stream; a heater provides trim energy. This is a synthetic teaching case, not a recommendation to operate a real process. Students must select reasonable illustrative numbers, label every invented number as an assumption, and keep all units, reference conditions and composition bases visible. The purpose is to assemble the unit's concepts into a consistent model that a reviewer can challenge.\n\n## Engineering question\n\nCalculate the sensible heat changes, inspect terminal temperature feasibility and construct an energy audit with a clearly declared time basis. State a single primary decision being supported, such as selecting a material-balance boundary, accepting a measurement, identifying a missing outlet or prioritizing an additional experiment. Do not use an unsupported plant-wide profit number or an unverified regulatory threshold to justify the decision.\n\n## Required evidence and calculation plan\n\n1. Write a process statement that defines input materials, expected outputs and the relevant boundaries. Distinguish a batch inventory from a flow and give the measurement time basis.\n2. Build an input data table with dimensions, source type, uncertainty estimate and acceptable bounds. Separate readings from engineering assumptions.\n3. Derive at least three equations rather than relying only on a calculator. For each, show a dimensional check and a feasible limiting case. Include a separate component balance when a concentration or reaction is involved.\n4. Carry out two contrasting cases: a nominal baseline and a plausible changed operating condition. If the changed condition violates the simplified model, stop and write that result as an important finding.\n5. Use the unit laboratories as computational demonstrations; export plots, retain input settings, and compare with a direct hand calculation. Plots are evidence about a mathematical model, not proof about an operating plant.\n6. Describe data or safety limitations. Include possible missing streams, property correlations, instrument bias, parameter dependence and common-cause uncertainty where they matter.\n7. Communicate the result as a reproducible engineering note with an executive statement, flow diagram, equations, tables, a source list and an open list of unresolved questions.\n\n## Review checkpoints\n\n- **Conservation:** material and energy accounting closure must be explicit; an unresolved residual is named, not silently forced to zero.\n- **Feasibility:** compositions must be physically bounded and equipment temperatures and pressures checked against the model's assumptions.\n- **Traceability:** a competent reader can identify exactly which values are assumed, measured or derived, and can reproduce the major quantities.\n- **Safety and ethics:** no classroom calculation is presented as industrial relief sizing, pharmaceutical validation, field authorization or environmental compliance proof.\n\n## Deliverables\n\nSubmit one concise process diagram, one independently checkable calculation sheet, two simulation exports, a one-page uncertainty/sensitivity commentary and a 250-word statement on what evidence would change your conclusion. Defend the chosen model scope and openly identify a more advanced analysis needed before industrial deployment."
