          "heading": "Engineering Audit, Diagnostic Reasoning & Assessment: Energy Foundations, Enthalpy and First Law Balances",
          "content": "# Unit 5 assessment: Energy foundations\n\nThese 12 problems combine quantitative reproduction, model validity, and audit methodology. Answers below are intended as diagnostic reasoning, not fabricated plant data.\n\n### Question 1: analysis of heat, work and energy inventories\n\nA well-insulated stirred tank is heated electrically with known power. Explain where electrical energy appears if temperature rises and a portion vaporizes.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 5.1. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 2: model validity\n\nEquating heat and temperature or treating a constant power input as a fixed quantity of energy. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 3: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: If heater input is 90 kW for 2 h, supplied energy is 180 kWh = 648 MJ. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 5: analysis of heat capacity and sensible heating\n\nA water stream at 1.5 kg/s warms from 20\u00b0C to 60\u00b0C. Compute ideal duty and discuss a possible correction for heat losses.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 5.2. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 6: model validity\n\nApplying a single gas heat capacity to an unknown multiphase or reacting mixture. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 7: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: Qdot = mdot Cp \u0394T; 2 kg/s \u00d7 4.2 kJ/(kg\u00b7K) \u00d7 15 K = 126 kW. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 9: analysis of phase change and enthalpy references\n\nA 200 kg/h stream warms from 25\u00b0C to 100\u00b0C and 25% evaporates. Form a two-part energy balance and identify needed property data.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 5.3. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 10: model validity\n\nTreating an entire liquid stream as vaporized because its bulk temperature reaches nominal boiling point. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 11: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: For 2 kg water vaporized using illustrative h_fg = 2250 kJ/kg, latent duty is 4500 kJ before preheating or losses. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena.\n\n### Question 13: analysis of heat exchangers and energy auditing\n\nAn exchanger heats cold water from 20 to 50\u00b0C using hot water from 90 to 65\u00b0C. Compute terminal differences for counterflow and discuss feasibility.\n\n**Answer guidance:** State the material or energy basis, identify which unknown can be inferred and which requires additional data, and use the equation derived in lesson 5.4. If the case is numerical, retain units during substitution and check conservation. If qualitative, give a testable comparison, not an unsupported claim of safety or feasibility.\n\n### Question 14: model validity\n\nUsing an outlet temperature that exceeds the hot inlet temperature without a heat pump or additional energy input. Identify the assumption violated by this shortcut and supply one physically plausible counterexample. Explain whether the shortcut creates a systematic overestimate or underestimate under the conditions you chose.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Begin with the definition of the reported quantity and its denominator; compare alternative physical bases. Where the bias direction depends on process conditions, state that dependence explicitly rather than forcing a single numerical answer.\n\n### Question 15: quantitative audit\n\nUse the lesson's worked equation: For a stream with mdot=1 kg/s, Cp=4 kJ/(kg K) and \u0394T=20 K, Qdot=80 kW. Perform an independent dimensional and limiting-case check. Explain what data would change the precision of the inferred result.\n\n**Answer guidance:** Check dimensions and conservation first. A good audit records measurement range, calibration status, uncertainties and separate estimates for unmodeled phenomena."
